{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_13","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '1,240p' /home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/code/combo_new/seed402918419/9/codegen_prompts/5.md\"","aggregated_output":"你要为一个新的 ARC-AGI 风格题目实现代码。代码文件必须包含 `main(grid: np.ndarray) -> np.ndarray` 和 `generate_input() -> np.ndarray`。\n\n## 新题位置\n\n- 描述目录：`/home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/seed402918419/9`\n- 题号：`5`\n- 目标代码路径：`/home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/code/combo_new/seed402918419/9/5.py`\n\n## 新题描述\n\n<new_puzzle index=\"5\">\n- `description`：网格中的多个形状对象各自拥有一个\"影子\"，需要根据全局光源位置判断哪些影子形状被篡改，并将被篡改的影子修复为与主体一致的正确形状。\n- `Input structure`：背景为 Azure。网格中有若干（3-5 个）彩色的实心小形状对象（3-5 像素的 4-连通块，形状各异如 L 形、T 形、直线形、方块形等），每个形状使用不同颜色（如 Red、Blue、Green、Yellow 等）。每个形状沿同一方向偏移固定距离处有一个 Grey 色的\"影子\"——与原形状形状理应完全一致的连通块。所有影子相对于其主体的偏移方向和偏移距离均相同（例如都向右下方偏移 3 格）。网格中有一个 Orange 单像素\"光源\"标记，位于所有形状的同一侧（例如所有形状的左上方）。大多数形状的 Grey 影子与形状完全一致（平移后像素集合重合），但有 1-2 个形状的影子被\"篡改\"——多了或少了一个像素，导致影子形状与主体不同。\n- `Rule`：\n  1. 找到 Orange 光源标记，确定其大致方位（位于所有形状群的哪个方向）。\n  2. 影子的偏移方向为光源方向的反方向（光源在左上方，则影子在主体的右下方）。通过观察多数正确的形状-影子对，确认具体的偏移向量（行偏移和列偏移）。\n  3. 对每个形状，将其像素集合按确认的偏移向量平移，得到\"正确影子\"的预期像素位置集合。\n  4. 将预期影子与实际 Grey 影子逐像素比较：如果完全一致则不做修改；如果不一致，则将实际的错误 Grey 影子像素全部擦除为背景色，并在预期位置绘制完整的正确 Grey 影子。\n  5. Orange 光源标记、所有彩色形状主体均保持不变。\n- `Key insight`：解题的第一层推理是从光源位置推断出全局统一的影子投射方向（反方向关系并非显而易见，需要从正确样例验证）；第二层是利用多数正确的影子对比来确认偏移向量并识别出少数被篡改的异常影子；第三层是将主体形状精确平移到影子位置进行修复。光源是方向线索，正确影子是规则校准器，篡改影子是待修复目标——三者的角色需要从样例中逐步理清。\n</new_puzzle>\n\n## 机制相似参考题\n\n下面参考题由本地相似度流程自动选择：先用 `annotations`/docstring/代码特征抽取机制标签，再按机制相似度、文本相似度和代码特征相似度重排，并用 MMR 做去重。优先学习这些题的对象识别方式、随机生成器约束、边界条件处理，不要照搬旧题规则。\n\n已选参考：eval/7b0280bc, train/ac0c2ac3, eval/31f7f899, train/e39e9282, train/230f2e48, train/6350f1f4, train/5ecac7f7, train/2faf500b\n\n<reference_puzzle rank=\"1\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"7b0280bc\">\n<similarity>\nscore=0.3321\nmechanism=0.4087\nlexical=0.0139\ncode_feature=0.4286\noverlap_tags=code:bbox, code:bfs, code:connected_components, object:line_segment, object:square, operation:bfs_distance, operation:repair_anomaly, operation:translate, output:same_size\n</similarity>\n<paths>\ncode=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/eval/7b0280bc.py\nannotation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/7b0280bc.json\n</paths>\n<description>\nRecolor the shortest path between two terminal squares in a square-and-track graph.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color.\n- Two non-bg colors form solid NxN squares (N>=2): one color marks exactly 2 terminals, the other marks stations.\n- A third non-bg color forms 1-cell-wide track segments connecting pairs of squares (graph edges).\n\nRule:\n- Find the shortest path (fewest edges) between the two terminal squares.\n- Recolor every station square on that path to green.\n- Recolor every track segment along that path to grey.\n- Everything else unchanged.\n\nKey insight: The grid encodes a graph where squares are nodes and tracks are edges; the task is graph shortest-path, not spatial proximity.\n</description>\n<annotation>\n{\n  \"type\": \"move_along_path\",\n  \"src\": \"small square with minority color\",\n  \"dst\": \"the other small square with minority color\",\n  \"path\": \"shortest path along path pixels (not squares and bg)\",\n  \"trajectory\": \"grey. recolor small squares with majority color on the way to green\"\n}\n</annotation>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle rank=\"2\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"ac0c2ac3\">\n<similarity>\nscore=0.3235\nmechanism=0.4375\nlexical=0.0101\ncode_feature=0.2000\noverlap_tags=code:bbox, object:marker, object:sprite, object:square, operation:bfs_distance, operation:translate\n</similarity>\n<paths>\ncode=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/train/ac0c2ac3.py\nannotation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/ac0c2ac3.json\n</paths>\n<description>\nFill each centered square ring with the color of the marker at that ring distance.\n\nInput structure\nThe input is an odd square grid with a dominant background color and one\nnon-background marker for every Chebyshev distance from the grid center.\nRule\nThe output replaces the background with concentric square rings centered on\nthe middle cell; every cell at distance d from the center takes the color of\nthe unique marker whose distance from the center is d.\nKey insight\nThe scattered markers are not objects to move; their Chebyshev distances\nform a palette for the centered square rings.\n</description>\n<annotation>\n{\n  \"type\": \"move_to_match\",\n  \"src\": \"non-background marker pixel s\",\n  \"dst\": \"centered square ring region d\",\n  \"match_cond\": \"s.chebyshev_distance_to_grid_center == d.ring_radius\",\n  \"transform\": \"fill every pixel in d with s.color\"\n}\n</annotation>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle rank=\"3\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"31f7f899\">\n<similarity>\nscore=0.3293\nmechanism=0.4226\nlexical=0.0098\ncode_feature=0.3333\noverlap_tags=code:bbox, code:connected_components, object:line_segment, object:sprite, operation:bfs_distance, operation:translate, output:same_size, relation:direction, relation:row_col\n</similarity>\n<paths>\ncode=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/eval/31f7f899.py\nannotation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/31f7f899.json\n</paths>\n<description>\nSort vertical bars by height and redistribute them across fixed colored slots.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg background; a horizontal base line of a single color spans the full grid width.\n- Several monochrome vertical bars of distinct colors rise upward from the base line.\n\nRule:\n- Rank bar shapes by height (shortest to tallest).\n- Rank original x-positions (slots) left to right.\n- Place the i-th shortest shape into the i-th leftmost slot, recoloring it to that slot's original color.\n- The base line is preserved unchanged.\n\nKey insight: Each slot keeps its x-position and color identity; only the bar silhouettes are redistributed among slots by height order.\n</description>\n<annotation>\n{\n  \"type\": \"move_to_match\",\n  \"src\": \"vertical bar sprite s\",\n  \"dst\": \"replace vertical bar d\",\n  \"match_cond\": \"s.size.order == d.pos.col.order\",\n  \"transform\": \"s.color -> d.color\"\n}\n</annotation>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle rank=\"4\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"e39e9282\">\n<similarity>\nscore=0.3103\nmechanism=0.3822\nlexical=0.0119\ncode_feature=0.4000\noverlap_tags=code:bbox, code:connected_components, object:line_segment, object:marker, object:sprite, object:square, operation:translate, relation:row_col\n</similarity>\n<paths>\ncode=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/train/e39e9282.py\nannotation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/e39e9282.json\n</paths>\n<description>\nProject maroon markers into adjacent grey or pink 3x3 squares, erasing grey squares afterward.\n\nInput structure\nThe grid has a dominant background, solid 3x3 grey and/or pink squares, and maroon marker pixels or segments directly touching one side of exactly one square.\nRule\nGrey squares are erased; their adjacent maroon markers stay in place and stamp the nearest edge cell inside the grey square. Pink squares stay pink; their adjacent maroon markers disappear and stamp the corresponding cell on the square's middle row or middle column.\nKey insight\nThe side touched by a marker determines the projection: grey uses the near edge, while pink uses the centerline, preserving the marker's coordinate along that side.\n</description>\n<annotation>\n{\n  \"type\": \"transform_in_place\",\n  \"src\": \"maroon marker pixels or segments adjacent to solid grey or pink 3x3 square objects\",\n  \"transform\": \"erase grey squares after stamping maroon into each touched near-edge cell; keep grey-adjacent markers in place; keep pink squares, remove pink-adjacent markers, and stamp their maroon cells onto the matching middle row or column of the pink square\"\n}\n</annotation>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle rank=\"5\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"230f2e48\">\n<similarity>\nscore=0.3157\nmechanism=0.3903\nlexical=0.0116\ncode_feature=0.40